@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Optional Laya shadow cross-check
+
+Triage retains its PRODUCT_BUG classification, investigation-confidence and repair-budget gates. After computing the candidate order, an opt-in client records QA cause hints in `TriageResult.system_one_advice`; pipeline console output shows the model label beside the trusted report label. Hints never alter selected/deferred/skipped findings or enter the coding prompt. They cannot authorize tools, dependency updates, secret rotation or merges.
+
+Enable only in the trusted service process by setting `FIXER_LAYA_URL` and `FIXER_LAYA_API_KEY`. Default model: `english`; optional `FIXER_LAYA_MODEL`. Optional `FIXER_LAYA_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` defaults to 2 and is bounded to 5 seconds. The adapter reads process environment variables, so adding these keys to a settings-only `.env` does not itself enable it. Leave them unset to disable it.
+
+At most eight report issues receive shadow advice. Unavailable/invalid responses return no hint and stop further shadow calls. Common credential forms and account emails are removed from bounded facts; approve that projection before enabling it for confidential reports. Never pass the API key to a coding subprocess. The existing allowlisted execution environment remains required.
+
+The real seven-case QA audit matched four references with English and missed the new card-required trial contract. Thus disagreement output is an observation for operators, not an automated QA gate. Current Bugsmith policy forbids dependency upgrades, so the 196 dependency findings require a separate remediation contract. The read-only planner in the sibling Laya project groups them into 34 repository/package investigation groups without resolving or closing issues.
+
+Guide: http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs/system-one-integrations.md. Laya's `examples/replay_shadow_adapters.py` exercises native clients with the captured reports, without invoking this repair pipeline. No deployment or GitHub mutation accompanies these local changes.
